/*!
    \page ascent-setup.html
    \title Project Setup and the Game Loop
    \previouspage Ascent
    \nextpage Provably Fair Randomness

    This chapter gets a window on screen and the multiplier curve behind it. By
    the end you will have an empty but running Felgo game, a C++ class that turns
    a duration into a multiplier, and that class talking to QML.

    \section1 Creating the Project

    Create a new Felgo project from Qt Creator's wizard, or start from an empty
    directory with a \c CMakeLists.txt. The Felgo-specific part of it is short:

    \snippet CMakeLists.txt felgo-project

    \c find_package(Felgo) brings in everything Felgo ships - the QML types, the
    plugins, the deployment helpers. The product variables underneath it are what
    end up in the app bundle: the identifier that identifies your app to the app
    stores, the version the store shows, and the license key. Leave the key empty
    while you build. It only removes the Felgo splash screen, and the splash
    screen turns out to be something we have to handle anyway.

    \c PRODUCT_STAGE stays at \c test until the very end, when switching it to
    \c publish is part of preparing a release build.

    \section1 The Game Window and the Scene

    A Felgo game starts with a \c GameWindow containing at least one \c Scene:

    \snippet qml/AscentMain.qml game-window

    Those two types are doing more than they look like. \c GameWindow is the
    application window, and \c screenWidth and \c screenHeight only decide how
    big it opens on a desktop - on a phone it fills the screen instead.

    \c Scene is where resolution independence happens. You give it a logical size
    once, here 320 by 480, and then position everything inside it in those
    coordinates forever. Felgo scales the scene to whatever display it lands on
    and letterboxes the remainder. That is why the background rectangle is
    anchored to \c fullWindowAnchorItem rather than to the scene: the scene is
    the play area, but the window may be taller or wider than it, and without
    that anchor the letterbox bars would show through as grey.

    Portrait, by the way, is not an arbitrary choice. The balloon rises, so
    vertical space is the playfield - and it is the right shape for a phone.

    \section1 From Elapsed Time to a Multiplier

    Now the first piece of actual game logic. A crash game's multiplier grows
    exponentially: slowly at the start, then faster and faster. That shape is
    what makes waiting stop feeling free at some point, which is the entire
    tension of the game.

    \snippet src/CrashCurve.cpp multiplier-at

    Two things are worth pointing out. The first is what the function does
    \e not do: it holds no state about the round. It does not know whether a
    round is running, when it started, or whether anyone has bet. You give it a
    duration and it gives you a multiplier. That makes it trivial to unit test -
    no clock to fake, no round to set up - and safe to call from a paint loop.

    The second is the guard on negative time. Clocks hand you odd deltas around a
    round boundary, and a negative one should not mean negative winnings. A round
    pays from 1.00x upwards, so anything before the start simply \e is the start.

    The growth rate is a property rather than a constant, so it can be tuned from
    QML while the game runs. At the default of 0.1 per second the multiplier
    doubles roughly every seven seconds, which is a pace players can feel.

    \section1 Letting QML See the Core

    The curve is a \c QObject with a \c Q_PROPERTY and a \c Q_INVOKABLE method,
    which is all QML needs. It just has to be told the type exists:

    \snippet main.cpp register-core-types

    Three of the four types are registered as creatable, so QML can write
    \c {CrashCurve {}} and get one. The round engine is not: it needs a curve and
    a wallet to mean anything, and a half-built engine conjured out of QML would
    be a bug waiting to happen. Registering it as uncreatable still exposes its
    states and properties to QML, which is exactly what the UI needs, while
    refusing to instantiate it.

    Build and run, and the window comes up with the multiplier the curve reports
    at ten seconds printed in it. It is not a game yet - nothing moves and there
    is nothing to press - but the two halves of the app are connected, and every
    chapter from here on adds to a running program.

    \include includes/chapters.qdocinc chapters
*/
